    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Which topologies admit which curvature?  Gauss–Bonnet decides. --- */\n/* For a closed surface of constant curvature K,  K·Area = 2πχ  (3.08),\n   so the SIGN of K is forced by the topology and nothing else. */\nchi(gen) := 2 - 2*gen$\nprint(\"closed orientable surfaces, constant curvature:\")$\nprint(\"   genus   χ    sign of K   model geometry\")$\nfor gen : 0 thru 4 do block([c],\n  c : chi(gen),\n  print(\"   \", gen, \"     \", c, \"    \",\n        if c > 0 then \" K > 0 \" elseif c = 0 then \" K = 0 \" else \" K < 0 \",\n        \"   \",\n        if c > 0 then \"S²\" elseif c = 0 then \"E²\" else \"H²\") )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Exactly one genus is spherical and exactly one is flat; every other\")$\nprint(\"closed orientable surface is hyperbolic.  Hyperbolic geometry is the\")$\nprint(\"generic case — which is why 6.04 spends its time there.\")$\n\n/* Areas, normalised to K = −1 */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"hyperbolic surfaces with K = −1:  Area = 2π|χ| = 4π(g−1)\")$\nprint(\"   genus    Gauss–Bonnet 2π|χ|      polygon area (4g−2)π − 2π\")$\nfor gen : 2 thru 5 do\n  print(\"   \", gen, \"        \", float(2*%pi*abs(chi(gen))),\n        \"          \", float((4*gen-2)*%pi - 2*%pi)) $\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The second column is independent: a genus-g surface is a regular\")$\nprint(\"hyperbolic 4g-gon with its sides glued, all 4g vertices meeting at one\")$\nprint(\"point so the angles sum to 2π, and the area of a hyperbolic m-gon is\")$\nprint(\"(m−2)π − Σangles.  Two unrelated routes, identical numbers.\")$\nprint(\"   genus 2: an octagon with all angles π/4, area = 6π − 2π = \",\n      float(4*%pi))$\n\n/* --- The complete lists in dimension 2 --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"COMPLETE 2-DIMENSIONAL SPACE FORMS (all quotients of a model):\")$\nprint(\"   K > 0 :  S²,  ℝP² = S²/±                              — 2 of them\")$\nprint(\"   K = 0 :  E²,  cylinder,  Möbius band,  torus,  Klein bottle\")$\nprint(\"                                                          — 5 of them\")$\nprint(\"   K < 0 :  infinitely many — every genus ≥ 2 surface, and\")$\nprint(\"            for each genus a whole moduli space of them\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The geometry is determined by K alone; the topology is the choice of\")$\nprint(\"discrete group Γ.  That is the Killing–Hopf theorem in one sentence.\")$\n\n/* --- Sanity: quotients really do inherit the curvature --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Quotients are LOCAL isometries, so curvature is inherited exactly:\")$\nprint(\"   flat torus ℝ²/ℤ²      : g = dx² + dy² in every chart, so K = 0\")$\nprint(\"   ℝP² = S²(1)/±         : K = 1, but diam = π/2, not π (4.06)\")$\nprint(\"Curvature is local; topology is global.  Constant curvature pins down\")$\nprint(\"the first completely and constrains — but does not determine — the second.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
