          "text": [
            "closed orientable surfaces, constant curvature:\n   genus   χ    sign of K   model geometry\n    0       2       K > 0      S²\n    1       0       K = 0      E²\n    2       -2       K < 0      H²\n    3       -4       K < 0      H²\n    4       -6       K < 0      H²\nExactly one genus is spherical and exactly one is flat; every other\nclosed orientable surface is hyperbolic.  Hyperbolic geometry is the\ngeneric case — which is why 6.04 spends its time there.\nhyperbolic surfaces with K = −1:  Area = 2π|χ| = 4π(g−1)\n   genus    Gauss–Bonnet 2π|χ|      polygon area (4g−2)π − 2π\n    2          12.566370614359172            12.566370614359172\n    3          25.132741228718345            25.132741228718345\n    4          37.69911184307752            37.69911184307752\n    5          50.26548245743669            50.26548245743669\nThe second column is independent: a genus-g surface is a regular\nhyperbolic 4g-gon with its sides glued, all 4g vertices meeting at one\npoint so the angles sum to 2π, and the area of a hyperbolic m-gon is\n(m−2)π − Σangles.  Two unrelated routes, identical numbers.\n   genus 2: an octagon with all angles π/4, area = 6π − 2π =\n  12.566370614359172\nCOMPLETE 2-DIMENSIONAL SPACE FORMS (all quotients of a model):\n   K > 0 :  S²,  ℝP² = S²/±                              — 2 of them\n   K = 0 :  E²,  cylinder,  Möbius band,  torus,  Klein bottle\n                                                          — 5 of them\n   K < 0 :  infinitely many — every genus ≥ 2 surface, and\n            for each genus a whole moduli space of them\nThe geometry is determined by K alone; the topology is the choice of\ndiscrete group Γ.  That is the Killing–Hopf theorem in one sentence.\nQuotients are LOCAL isometries, so curvature is inherited exactly:\n   flat torus ℝ²/ℤ²      : g = dx² + dy² in every chart, so K = 0\n   ℝP² = S²(1)/±         : K = 1, but diam = π/2, not π (4.06)\nCurvature is local; topology is global.  Constant curvature pins down\nthe first completely and constrains — but does not determine — the second."
