    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$R_{ijkl} = K\\,(g_{ik}g_{jl} - g_{il}g_{jk}) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad \\dim\\mathrm{Isom}(M) = \\tfrac12 n(n+1)$$\n\n**The model form, checked rather than asserted.** Both sign conventions were tested against the computed Riemann tensor for $S^2(R_0)$, $H^2$, $E^2$ and $S^3$. With the lowered index in the **last** slot — this repo's convention since 4.04 — the fit is the *negative* of the textbook expression, with zero mismatches out of 16 (and 81 for $S^3$). The textbook form lowers into the first slot. Same tensor, same $K$; the sign is bookkeeping, and 4.04 was caught by exactly this before.\n\n**Stage 2's three groups, recovered from three metrics.** The Killing equation $\\mathcal{L}_Xg = 0$ was solved by verification on each model, and each has a 3-dimensional solution space — the maximum $\\tfrac12 n(n+1)$ for $n=2$:\n\n| model | Killing fields | group | built in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $\\mathbb{E}^2$ | $\\partial_x,\\ \\partial_y,\\ -y\\partial_x + x\\partial_y$ | $E(2)$ | 1.02–1.04 |\n| $S^2$ | the three axis rotations | $SO(3)$ | 2.07 |\n| $H^2$ | $1,\\ z,\\ z^2$ in $z = x+iy$ | $PSL_2(\\mathbb{R})$ | 2.06 |\n\nThe hyperbolic case is the sharpest. The fields $1, z, z^2$ have flows $z \\mapsto z+t$, $z \\mapsto e^tz$, $z \\mapsto z/(1-tz)$ — Möbius transformations, which 2.06 derived from circle geometry with no metric anywhere in the argument. **Klein's group and Riemann's metric are two descriptions of one object.**\n\nThe torus of revolution is the control: its rotation field is Killing, but $\\partial_u$ is not, and its curvature is non-constant (4.06). Maximal symmetry and constant curvature stand or fall together.\n\n**Gauss–Bonnet decides the topology.** For a closed surface $K\\cdot\\mathrm{Area} = 2\\pi\\chi$, so the sign of $K$ is fixed by the genus and nothing else: genus 0 spherical, genus 1 flat, **every other closed orientable surface hyperbolic**. Hyperbolic geometry is not an exotic case but the generic one.\n\nThe areas were computed twice by unrelated routes — $2\\pi|\\chi| = 4\\pi(g-1)$ from Gauss–Bonnet, and $(4g-2)\\pi - 2\\pi$ from the hyperbolic area of the regular $4g$-gon whose sides are glued — with identical output at every genus. Genus 2: an octagon with all angles $\\pi/4$, area $4\\pi$.\n\n**The complete lists in dimension 2.** Two spherical space forms ($S^2$, $\\mathbb{RP}^2$); five flat ones ($\\mathbb{E}^2$, cylinder, Möbius band, torus, Klein bottle); infinitely many hyperbolic, with a moduli space at each genus. The geometry is $K$; the topology is $\\Gamma$.\n\n**Stage 4 closes here.** It began with a metric and no ambient space (4.01), built the connection it forces (4.02), the geodesics that connection defines (4.03), the curvature that survives coordinate change (4.04), the traces that make curvature usable (4.05), and the comparison theorems that turn curvature bounds into topology (4.06). This notebook shows that in the maximally symmetric case the whole apparatus collapses back onto the three geometries of Stage 2.\n\n## Next\n\n**Stage 5 — Lie Groups, Bundles and Curvature as a Field** takes the isometry groups seriously as geometric objects. The Killing fields computed here are the Lie algebra of $\\mathrm{Isom}(M)$; Stage 5 gives them their own geometry, and reinterprets curvature as the field strength of a connection on a principal bundle — the language in which gauge theory and general relativity are the same construction.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 8 for the constant-curvature form of $Rm$ and Schur's lemma; Chapter 3 §\"Model Riemannian Manifolds\" for the three models and their isometry groups.\n- Wolf, J.A. (2011) *Spaces of Constant Curvature*. 6th edn. Providence: AMS Chelsea. The classification of space forms in full, in every dimension. This is the reference the Killing–Hopf theorem lives in.\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. Chapters 8–9 for the transformation-group view of the same three geometries, at the level Stage 2 works at.\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. Chapter 7 for non-Euclidean geometry reached axiomatically rather than metrically — the third route to the same list.\n- Thurston, W.P. (1997) *Three-Dimensional Geometry and Topology*. Princeton: Princeton University Press. Where this story goes in dimension 3, and the setting for 6.04.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. The sign of the constant-curvature form was determined by testing both options against computed output, not by recall.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
